  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between Tenacissoside B and the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14, as it is indirectly targeted through multiple pathways involving the Prelamin-A/C protein. Tenacissoside B appears to act as a target for several compounds, including 5-Oxobrucine, Sasanquasaponin I, and Cassialactone, all of which are linked to the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14. These compounds are likely acting as modulators or inhibitors of the proteasome, which is a critical component of the cellular protein degradation machinery. The shared target of these compounds with Prelamin-A/C indicates a possible mechanism of action involving the proteasome, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be elucidated. The evidence is consistent across different pathways, suggesting a possible role for Tenacissoside B in modulating proteasome activity, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct interaction."
